Those sound like different types of covariate; only 'sex' is potentially a 'group' as the word is used in capture-recapture. 'habitat' sounds like a mask covariate that might be used to model density in 'secr' but not 'openCR' (I infer you are doing an open population analysis as you refer to survival). 'Season' is possibly a session covariate in either package. I suggest you build the model one step at a time, starting with the covariate that is most important to you.

Also, please start a new topic unless your question relates directly to the subject.

On Thursday, April 29, 2021 at 9:50:44 PM UTC+12 chidod...@gmail.com wrote:
> Dear Murray,
> I am having a data that I want to look on survival of rodents, I managed to have captured the history, but the place where I'm stuck is the group factor. I have more than three, which are habitat, Season, and sex with weight as covariates. But all tutorials have only one group factors, please can I get a script or guide on how to go through this I will be gratefull.
> Regard Chidodo
>
> On Mon, 26 Apr 2021, 00:18 Murray Efford, <murray...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Can you please be a bit more precise - presumably your call was something like secr.fit(CH, model = list(pID~daynight,...), ...) (pID is not an argument of secr.fit; 'session' has a special meaning in 'secr')? What sort of covariate was your 'session'? What were the issues? Do you really have a case for bootstrapping? I'm not sure what works for pID, but I suspect yes you would need to make it a time covariate (the direction you're heading) or a traps covariate.
